========================================================================================
PART 1: look-elsewhere on the Ward Clebsch 1/sqrt(C)
========================================================================================
  The note derives C = 2*N_c = 6 (color 3 x isospin 2). Test which O(1)
  integer denominators land a 'top-like' m_t in [150,200] GeV.
     C   1/sqrt(C)   y_t(M_Pl)   m_t [GeV]  top-like?
     1     1.00000     1.06741     209.864      False
     2     0.70711     0.75477     199.445       True
     3     0.57735     0.61627     190.484       True
     4     0.50000     0.53371     182.648       True
     5     0.44721     0.47736     175.714       True
     6     0.40825     0.43577     169.516       True
     7     0.37796     0.40344     163.932       True
     8     0.35355     0.37739     158.865       True
     9     0.33333     0.35580     154.240       True
    10     0.31623     0.33754     149.996      False
    12     0.28868     0.30813     142.461      False
  [PASS] C=6 lands top-like
  ==> 8 of 11 tested O(1) denominators give a top-like m_t.
  ==> Look-elsewhere factor is MODEST: the QFP focusing compresses a broad
      UV range, so several C give 150-200 GeV. C=6 is NOT uniquely forced by
      the target; it IS forced by the sqrt(2 N_c) group theory. Distinguish:
      the VALUE is group-theoretic; its AGREEMENT is not a sharp discriminator.

========================================================================================
PART 2: pole vs MSbar -- what IS the 169.5 number?
========================================================================================
  m_t = y_t(v)*v/sqrt(2) is the tree relation at scale mu=v, i.e. a
  RUNNING (MSbar-like) top mass near v, NOT the pole mass. The chain
  compares it directly to the POLE mass 172.69. Standard QCD pole/MSbar:
    m_pole = m_MSbar(m_t) * (1 + 4/3 * alpha_s/pi + ...)  ~ +6% at top.
  m_t(running, at v) = 169.52 GeV
  one-loop QCD pole shift factor ~ 1.0568
  => if 169.5 is treated as MSbar(v): pole ~ 179.14 GeV (dev vs 172.69: +3.73%)
  => if 169.5 is treated as the pole already: dev -1.84%
  => vs MSbar m_t(m_t)~162.5: dev +4.32%
  [PASS] pole-vs-MSbar scheme ambiguity is >= the quoted 1.8% deviation  (scheme choice moves m_t by several %)

