  Band A -- LOCAL systematic (alpha_bare fixed at derived 1/4pi; vary
            <P> within MC sigma, 1-vs-2 loop):  [165.48, 170.24] GeV
            => m_t = 169.5  +0.7/-4.0 GeV (+0.43/-2.38%)
  Band B -- SCHEME axis (is 169.5 the pole or a running mass?):
            [169.52, 179.14] GeV  (treat-as-pole vs MSbar->pole)
  Axis C -- alpha_bare HYPERSENSITIVITY (structural, NOT a free input):
            a +-2% move in alpha_bare swings m_t to [121.4, 235.1] GeV
            because v ~ alpha_LM^16. m_t INHERITS the hierarchy magnitude;
            the alpha_LM-magnitude open gate is therefore load-bearing for m_t.

  HONEST HEADLINE BAND (local syst + scheme) = [165.5, 179.1] GeV
  observed pole m_t = 172.69 GeV lies INSIDE this band.
  [PASS] observed pole m_t inside the (local-syst + scheme) band  ([165.5,179.1] vs 172.69)
  [PASS] the -1.84% headline is scheme-dependent (not robust to pole-vs-running)  (flipping scheme moves the central deviation by >2%)
  [PASS] m_t is hypersensitive to alpha_bare via the v ~ alpha_LM^16 hierarchy  (+-2% alpha_bare -> >+-30% m_t; the hierarchy gate is load-bearing)

========================================================================================
PART 4: QFP insensitivity -- does the UV Ward boundary even matter?
========================================================================================
  Vary y_t(M_Pl) by hand (decouple from the chain) and read off m_t,
  to test the chain's claim that a 10% UV change -> <0.5% IR change.
   y_t(M_Pl)    y_t(v)  m_t[GeV]
      0.3000   0.80513   140.214
      0.4000   0.93766   163.292
      0.4358   0.97342   169.521
      0.5000   1.02603   178.683
      0.7000   1.12771   196.390
      1.0000   1.19614   208.307
      2.0000   1.25499   218.555
      5.0000   1.27456   221.964
  Near baseline: a 23% UV change moves m_t by 9.08% (15.39 GeV).
  [PASS] UV Ward boundary IS load-bearing at y_t~0.44 (not washed out)  (at low y_t the QFP does NOT fully attract; UV value matters)
  KEY: the IR quasi-FP only fully attracts for LARGE UV y_t (>~1), which
  lands m_t~218 (the no-go band). At the framework's UV value ~0.44 the
  result is SENSITIVE to the UV boundary -> the prediction genuinely rests
  on y_t(M_Pl)=g_lattice/sqrt(6), i.e. on the Ward boundary being derived.

